WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.302
Machine learning models often look excellent on
benchmark datasets,

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:07.703
then break the moment they hit real production data.

00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:13.065
This paper by Matthew Groh at the MIT Media Lab
argues that the usual explanation,

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:15.616
distribution shift, is the wrong frame.

00:00:15.696 --> 00:00:20.979
Distribution shift only measures that two datasets
differ; it never tells you why.

00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:22.941
Groh introduces context shift,

00:00:23.021 --> 00:00:27.584
the semantically meaningful changes in how data are
created, collected,

00:00:27.664 --> 00:00:30.658
and curated that actually drive those failures,

00:00:30.738 --> 00:00:36.937
and lays out three concrete ways to address it:
leaning on human intuition and expert knowledge,

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:41.580
building dynamic benchmarks, and clearly stating a
model's limitations.
